<clickhouse>
  <!-- Bound ClickHouse's OWN telemetry. Companion to clickhouse-memory.xml, which
       caps memory on this 24 GB host; this file caps the other two resources the
       same box was losing: CPU and disk.

       Measured 2026-09-19, container up 5 days:

         system.*  (ClickHouse's own debug telemetry)  81.87 GiB   4,449,899,327 rows
         Langfuse  (the data you actually keep)           223.50 MiB       732,090 rows

       No system log table had a TTL, so they grew without bound, and ClickHouse sat
       at 242-1396% CPU perpetually merging system.text_log and system.part_log.
       From the host it looked like "the Docker VM is burning cores"; the VM was
       innocent, this was. A week later text_log had grown from 27 to 48 GiB.

       Three levers, in order of impact:

       1. text_log <level>: the shipped default is `trace`, i.e. every server log
          line at maximum verbosity is INSERTed into a MergeTree table. That is the
          ingest rate behind the merges. `warning` keeps what is worth reading.
       2. trace_log removed: query-profiler stack samples, 50 GiB, never read in a
          local Langfuse. Re-enable by deleting the <trace_log remove="1"/> line.
       3. A 3-day TTL on every remaining log table.

       Gotchas, both hit while writing this file:
       - opentelemetry_span_log is left alone on purpose. It is the one table the
         shipped config gives an explicit <engine>, and ClickHouse refuses to start
         (Code 36, restart loop) if a sibling <ttl> is added next to an <engine>.
         It is small (~57 MiB). processors_profile_log already ships with a TTL.
       - Do NOT apply retention with ALTER TABLE ... MODIFY TTL instead: on start,
         ClickHouse compares each system log table against the definition built
         from this config and, if they differ, renames the old table aside
         (text_log -> text_log_0) and creates a fresh one. Retention has to live
         here to survive a restart. Those renamed *_log_N tables are dead weight;
         drop them after a config change. -->

  <trace_log remove="1"/>

  <text_log>
    <level>warning</level>
    <ttl>event_date + INTERVAL 3 DAY DELETE</ttl>
  </text_log>

  <part_log><ttl>event_date + INTERVAL 3 DAY DELETE</ttl></part_log>
  <metric_log><ttl>event_date + INTERVAL 3 DAY DELETE</ttl></metric_log>
  <asynchronous_metric_log><ttl>event_date + INTERVAL 3 DAY DELETE</ttl></asynchronous_metric_log>
  <query_log><ttl>event_date + INTERVAL 3 DAY DELETE</ttl></query_log>
  <query_thread_log><ttl>event_date + INTERVAL 3 DAY DELETE</ttl></query_thread_log>
  <error_log><ttl>event_date + INTERVAL 3 DAY DELETE</ttl></error_log>
  <query_metric_log><ttl>event_date + INTERVAL 3 DAY DELETE</ttl></query_metric_log>
  <query_views_log><ttl>event_date + INTERVAL 3 DAY DELETE</ttl></query_views_log>
</clickhouse>
